# Cloudflare Pages serves this file as response headers for the paths below.
#
# The API cannot carry these for the site: the browser's own rules about which
# scripts, frames and styles a page may load apply to the document that loads
# them, and that document comes from Pages. Everything listed here was derived
# from what the built app actually fetches, not from a template - a directive
# that guesses wrong does not warn, it silently breaks sign-in or fonts.
#
#   script-src   the app's own bundle, plus Google's sign-in library, which the
#                page injects as a <script> and which has no other origin.
#   frame-src    the One Tap / button iframe inside accounts.google.com.
#   style-src    Google's stylesheet for Public Sans, and Google's own button
#                stylesheet. 'unsafe-inline' is here because the sign-in library
#                writes style attributes into the button it builds in this page:
#                without it the button renders unstyled and unreadable. That was
#                observed, not assumed - the first version of this policy left it
#                out and gsi/client logged four blocked inline-style violations.
#                It costs the least of the three directives: script-src carries
#                neither 'unsafe-inline' nor 'unsafe-eval', and both an injected
#                inline script and a direct eval() were confirmed blocked.
#   font-src     the .woff2 files live on fonts.gstatic.com, not googleapis.com.
#   img-src      blob: because the reader turns page images into object URLs
#                after an authenticated fetch; without it every page is blank.
#   connect-src  the API (XHR and the authenticated blob fetches) and the
#                worker's health URL, which the browser pings to wake it.
#   frame-ancestors 'none', object-src 'none', base-uri 'self',
#   form-action 'self'  no embedding, no plugins, no <base> hijack, and no
#                sending this page's forms to someone else's endpoint.
#
# Local `npm run dev` is not covered: Vite does not read _headers. The CSP is
# verified against the production origin, where the third-party allow-lists
# (Google's authorized-javascript-origins rule) actually apply.
#
# Cache-Control is set for /assets/* and deliberately nowhere else. Pages does
# not let the later, more specific block win the way this file once assumed: it
# combines every rule whose pattern matches the requested path, so a
# Cache-Control on /* also lands on the bundle and the two values ship glued
# together - "no-store, public, max-age=31536000, immutable" - which a browser
# reads as no-store. Measured on the deployed site, not inferred.
#
# The patterns name paths, not files, because Pages matches against the URL the
# visitor asked for: a rule called /index.html would miss /admin, /login and
# every other deep link. Those documents keep Pages' own default,
# "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate", which still revalidates on every
# navigation, so a deploy cannot leave cached HTML naming an old bundle.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://accounts.google.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://accounts.google.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' blob: data:; connect-src 'self' https://gyansankalp-api.onrender.com https://gyansankalp-worker.onrender.com; frame-src https://accounts.google.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), interest-cohort=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

/assets/*
  # Vite fingerprints every file here, so a new build is a new URL.
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
